--odysseyCAPboundary7
Content-Type: application/json; charset=UTF-8

{"snippet": {"videoId": "XepQsg7phAo", "language": "en", "name": "", "isDraft": false}}
--odysseyCAPboundary7
Content-Type: application/octet-stream

1
00:00:00,589 --> 00:00:02,889
Why did people invent writing?

2
00:00:03,173 --> 00:00:06,447
Look at the last receipt you were handed.
That's why.

3
00:00:06,876 --> 00:00:10,519
The oldest
writing on Earth is mostly bookkeeping.

4
00:00:10,586 --> 00:00:15,699
Over 6,000 clay tablets,
most from one city in southern Iraq.

5
00:00:15,766 --> 00:00:16,844
Uruk.

6
00:00:16,911 --> 00:00:20,351
On 18 of them, one name keeps coming back.

7
00:00:20,418 --> 00:00:21,504
Kushim.

8
00:00:22,035 --> 00:00:25,162
It may be the oldest
name of a person we can read.

9
00:00:25,229 --> 00:00:28,160
And it belongs to someone
who counted barley.

10
00:00:28,227 --> 00:00:33,294
Nearly a square mile of mud brick.
Tens of thousands of people.

11
00:00:33,361 --> 00:00:36,647
You're about to live here,
5,000 years ago.

12
00:00:36,714 --> 00:00:39,300
Would you survive the world's first city?

13
00:00:40,211 --> 00:00:44,019
Here's the game.
Five moments from your life in the city.

14
00:00:44,086 --> 00:00:47,747
Before each one, make your guess.
Would you make it?

15
00:00:47,884 --> 00:00:52,240
And on the right, five things the world
has been told about Uruk.

16
00:00:52,307 --> 00:00:55,168
Let's see which of them
survive the evidence.

17
00:00:55,235 --> 00:00:58,361
First question. Do you even get a house?

18
00:00:58,563 --> 00:01:00,078
Make your guess.

19
00:01:01,111 --> 00:01:05,472
The town in our last film would
fit inside it nearly twenty times.

20
00:01:05,539 --> 00:01:10,756
For thousands of years, settlements here
rarely grew bigger than a few city blocks.

21
00:01:10,823 --> 00:01:16,110
Then, within a few centuries,
this one spread over nearly a square mile.

22
00:01:16,177 --> 00:01:18,574
One idea is that marshes were turning

23
00:01:18,641 --> 00:01:22,678
into farmland just as people
poured in from the countryside.

24
00:01:22,745 --> 00:01:25,188
So where do all those people live?

25
00:01:25,319 --> 00:01:29,198
German archaeologists have been
digging Uruk since 1912.

26
00:01:29,265 --> 00:01:34,901
In 2014, Harvard archaeologist Jason
Ur pointed out something strange.

27
00:01:34,968 --> 00:01:36,259
In all that time,

28
00:01:36,326 --> 00:01:40,113
nobody has excavated a single
ordinary house of the period here.

29
00:01:40,180 --> 00:01:42,407
We know the giant buildings in the middle.

30
00:01:42,474 --> 00:01:45,692
The homes of the people who
built them are still missing.

31
00:01:45,759 --> 00:01:47,802
So what did your house look like?

32
00:01:47,869 --> 00:01:51,159
To see it,
we have to travel far up the Euphrates,

33
00:01:51,226 --> 00:01:53,377
to a colony in today's Syria.

34
00:01:53,444 --> 00:01:56,234
Today that town lies under a reservoir.

35
00:01:56,301 --> 00:02:00,021
But before the water came,
archaeologists dug it up.

36
00:02:00,088 --> 00:02:05,084
There, the houses have the same plan
as the giant buildings, only smaller.

37
00:02:05,151 --> 00:02:11,013
A long central hall, rooms on both sides,
and your own walled courtyard.

38
00:02:11,080 --> 00:02:14,839
The bigger ones even had a reception hall,
for guests.

39
00:02:14,906 --> 00:02:17,125
Verdict: you get a house.

40
00:02:17,192 --> 00:02:19,920
We just can't show
you one from Uruk itself.

41
00:02:20,003 --> 00:02:24,079
This is the first stop of a new season:
the first cities.

42
00:02:24,146 --> 00:02:27,314
Every Wednesday and Saturday,
we go one step further.

43
00:02:27,381 --> 00:02:30,167
Subscribe,
and see if you'd survive the next one.

44
00:02:30,234 --> 00:02:32,837
Second question. What's your job?

45
00:02:32,975 --> 00:02:34,489
Make your guess.

46
00:02:35,523 --> 00:02:38,447
In this city, someone wrote the jobs down.

47
00:02:38,514 --> 00:02:39,588
In a list.

48
00:02:39,655 --> 00:02:42,874
It's one of the oldest
lists of professions in the world,

49
00:02:42,941 --> 00:02:45,791
from about 3200 BC.

50
00:02:45,858 --> 00:02:47,277
And it's ranked.

51
00:02:47,344 --> 00:02:51,342
The first entry is a top official,
maybe the ruler himself.

52
00:02:51,409 --> 00:02:55,337
Then dozens more titles,
down and down the list.

53
00:02:55,404 --> 00:02:58,622
Students copied this list,
almost unchanged,

54
00:02:58,689 --> 00:03:00,844
for about a thousand years.

55
00:03:00,911 --> 00:03:03,558
Where would you be on it?
Probably nowhere.

56
00:03:03,625 --> 00:03:05,833
The list is for people with titles.

57
00:03:05,900 --> 00:03:07,839
Because the city needed hands.

58
00:03:07,906 --> 00:03:12,553
For canals, fields, bricks,
and the giant buildings in the middle.

59
00:03:12,620 --> 00:03:14,911
And someone had to make the bowls.

60
00:03:14,978 --> 00:03:17,889
These rough bowls turn
up by the thousands.

61
00:03:17,956 --> 00:03:21,795
Made fast, in moulds,
and thrown away by the heap.

62
00:03:21,862 --> 00:03:25,019
Why would a city need so many cheap bowls?

63
00:03:25,221 --> 00:03:28,331
Archaeologists call
them bevelled-rim bowls.

64
00:03:28,398 --> 00:03:31,333
They're one of the most
common objects of the age.

65
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:32,610
Here's a clue.

66
00:03:32,677 --> 00:03:35,895
On the tablets,
the sign for a ration is a human

67
00:03:35,962 --> 00:03:38,731
head with one of these bowls at its mouth.

68
00:03:38,798 --> 00:03:44,411
In 1970, archaeologist Hans Nissen
made the case that has led ever since.

69
00:03:44,478 --> 00:03:47,708
They held your wages. A ration of barley.

70
00:03:47,775 --> 00:03:51,462
Verdict: you work with your hands,
too low for the list.

71
00:03:51,529 --> 00:03:54,726
And your pay comes in a bowl. Or does it?

72
00:03:55,364 --> 00:03:58,091
Third question. Do you eat well?

73
00:03:58,226 --> 00:03:59,740
Make your guess.

74
00:04:00,776 --> 00:04:03,567
Look at the city's most famous object.

75
00:04:03,634 --> 00:04:07,067
A vase of carved stone,
about a metre tall.

76
00:04:07,134 --> 00:04:10,016
At the bottom, water. Then crops.

77
00:04:10,083 --> 00:04:11,164
Then sheep.

78
00:04:11,231 --> 00:04:15,028
That's your menu.
Bread, porridge and beer from barley.

79
00:04:15,095 --> 00:04:19,095
Dates. Milk from the flocks.
Fish from the canals.

80
00:04:19,162 --> 00:04:21,819
But what was really in those bowls?

81
00:04:22,020 --> 00:04:26,364
In 2022, a team tested the residue
inside ten of them,

82
00:04:26,431 --> 00:04:29,366
from a site of the same age
in northern Iraq.

83
00:04:29,433 --> 00:04:31,905
Inside, they found traces of meat.

84
00:04:31,972 --> 00:04:34,759
Probably stews, and maybe dairy.

85
00:04:34,826 --> 00:04:41,472
Then, in 2026, the same project tested
37 vessels from the big buildings there.

86
00:04:41,539 --> 00:04:45,210
Meat from cattle,
sheep or goats, and probably pigs.

87
00:04:45,277 --> 00:04:48,021
Fish from the river. Plants and dairy.

88
00:04:48,088 --> 00:04:52,306
Meat was eaten far more often than the old
texts made us think.

89
00:04:52,373 --> 00:04:55,924
And there was one more ingredient.
Pine resin.

90
00:04:55,991 --> 00:04:59,850
They flavoured
or cooked their meals with tree resin.

91
00:04:59,917 --> 00:05:02,863
Verdict:
you eat better than the textbooks said.

92
00:05:02,930 --> 00:05:04,801
With a taste of pine.

93
00:05:04,886 --> 00:05:08,445
Fourth question.
Does the city know you exist?

94
00:05:08,580 --> 00:05:10,094
Make your guess.

95
00:05:11,116 --> 00:05:12,692
Remember those tablets?

96
00:05:12,759 --> 00:05:16,046
They were made right here,
in the big buildings in the middle.

97
00:05:16,113 --> 00:05:19,546
The expert Robert Englund
spent decades reading them.

98
00:05:19,613 --> 00:05:22,447
The first ones, he found,
only have numbers.

99
00:05:22,514 --> 00:05:25,380
Around 3300 BC.

100
00:05:25,447 --> 00:05:29,181
Then, signs for things. Barley. Beer.

101
00:05:29,248 --> 00:05:31,737
Sheep. Cloth. Workers.

102
00:05:31,804 --> 00:05:33,870
Barley in, barley out.

103
00:05:33,937 --> 00:05:37,443
The city kept count of what
it gave and what it was owed.

104
00:05:37,510 --> 00:05:38,800
So who was Kushim?

105
00:05:38,867 --> 00:05:44,481
One tablet reads, roughly:
28,086 measures of barley.

106
00:05:44,548 --> 00:05:47,065
37 months. Kushim.

107
00:05:47,725 --> 00:05:51,869
Whoever Kushim was,
you did not want to lose that barley.

108
00:05:51,936 --> 00:05:53,269
But here's the catch.

109
00:05:53,336 --> 00:05:57,836
Experts still argue whether
Kushim was a person, or a job title.

110
00:05:57,903 --> 00:05:58,711
And you?

111
00:05:58,778 --> 00:06:02,209
If you got barley or beer,
you were probably counted.

112
00:06:02,276 --> 00:06:07,402
To mark goods and deals, people rolled
a little stone cylinder across wet clay.

113
00:06:07,469 --> 00:06:09,185
A kind of signature.

114
00:06:09,252 --> 00:06:14,044
Excavators found many thousands of those
seal marks in the middle of the city.

115
00:06:14,111 --> 00:06:16,915
Verdict: the city knows you exist.

116
00:06:16,982 --> 00:06:19,586
As a number, in someone's account.

117
00:06:19,667 --> 00:06:20,744
Last question.

118
00:06:20,811 --> 00:06:24,111
Who's in charge, and do you have to obey?

119
00:06:24,253 --> 00:06:25,767
Make your guess.

120
00:06:26,803 --> 00:06:28,875
Walk into the middle of the city.

121
00:06:28,942 --> 00:06:33,730
Columns covered in thousands of coloured
clay cones, like a mosaic.

122
00:06:33,797 --> 00:06:38,658
And on a platform about twelve
metres high, a building painted white.

123
00:06:38,725 --> 00:06:41,807
For a century, we called these temples.

124
00:06:41,874 --> 00:06:43,317
But were they?

125
00:06:43,825 --> 00:06:47,827
Their plans are the same as the houses,
only far bigger.

126
00:06:47,894 --> 00:06:49,791
Jason Ur puts it simply.

127
00:06:49,858 --> 00:06:53,720
The difference between a house
and a temple here is one of degree,

128
00:06:53,787 --> 00:06:54,865
not kind.

129
00:06:54,932 --> 00:06:57,508
So many archaeologists
now think some of them

130
00:06:57,575 --> 00:07:02,384
were the great households of the powerful.
Some may still have been for the gods.

131
00:07:02,451 --> 00:07:05,910
So who gives the orders?
Look at the vase again.

132
00:07:05,977 --> 00:07:10,474
In the middle, a line of naked
men carrying baskets and jars.

133
00:07:10,541 --> 00:07:14,600
At the top, a woman,
or a goddess, receives the gifts.

134
00:07:14,667 --> 00:07:18,312
Facing her stood someone important,
probably the ruler.

135
00:07:18,379 --> 00:07:20,270
Most of him has broken away.

136
00:07:20,337 --> 00:07:24,699
What survives is the end of his long belt,
carried by a servant.

137
00:07:24,766 --> 00:07:27,350
That's the order
of the city in one picture.

138
00:07:27,417 --> 00:07:30,213
And you're one of the men
carrying the baskets.

139
00:07:30,280 --> 00:07:33,402
But were you even free? Not everyone was.

140
00:07:33,469 --> 00:07:39,188
Another tablet from around 3100
BC names a man called Gal-Sal,

141
00:07:39,255 --> 00:07:42,427
and his two slaves. A man and a woman.

142
00:07:42,494 --> 00:07:46,794
Some of the oldest names we can
read belong to people who were owned.

143
00:07:47,629 --> 00:07:51,482
Verdict: someone is in charge.
You do the carrying.

144
00:07:51,549 --> 00:07:54,635
And if your luck runs out, you're owned.

145
00:07:54,800 --> 00:07:57,593
Before we finish,
there's someone you should meet.

146
00:07:57,660 --> 00:08:02,239
This face was carved in Uruk
around 3100 BC.

147
00:08:02,306 --> 00:08:05,600
White marble,
about the size of a real face.

148
00:08:05,667 --> 00:08:07,843
Who was she? Nobody knows.

149
00:08:07,910 --> 00:08:11,411
A goddess, a priestess,
or a woman of the city.

150
00:08:11,478 --> 00:08:16,484
Her eyes and eyebrows were once inlaid,
maybe with shell and blue stone.

151
00:08:16,551 --> 00:08:19,763
She probably had a body of wood, now gone.

152
00:08:19,830 --> 00:08:25,334
In 2003, during the war, she was stolen
from the Iraq Museum in Baghdad.

153
00:08:25,401 --> 00:08:26,477
That September,

154
00:08:26,544 --> 00:08:31,129
she was found buried in a farmer's field,
undamaged, and brought home.

155
00:08:31,196 --> 00:08:33,388
Same city. Same museum.

156
00:08:33,455 --> 00:08:39,255
The Warka Vase was looted that April too,
and handed back that June, in pieces.

157
00:08:39,322 --> 00:08:42,849
5,000 years old, and still surviving.

158
00:08:43,959 --> 00:08:45,616
So how did you do?

159
00:08:45,822 --> 00:08:48,814
Home: yes, though we can't show you one.

160
00:08:48,881 --> 00:08:50,979
Work: too low for the list.

161
00:08:51,046 --> 00:08:53,932
Food: better than we thought, with pine.

162
00:08:53,999 --> 00:08:55,673
Counted: as a number.

163
00:08:55,740 --> 00:08:58,532
The bosses: you carry their baskets.

164
00:08:58,599 --> 00:09:00,399
And the list on the right?

165
00:09:00,604 --> 00:09:02,174
The first city ever?

166
00:09:02,241 --> 00:09:07,034
Another giant settlement, Tell Brak,
grew in the north at about the same time.

167
00:09:07,101 --> 00:09:09,178
So Uruk shares the title.

168
00:09:09,245 --> 00:09:12,190
Bowls of barley: rewritten by the lab.

169
00:09:12,257 --> 00:09:15,146
Giant temples: maybe palaces.

170
00:09:15,213 --> 00:09:17,793
Kushim: maybe a job title.

171
00:09:17,860 --> 00:09:19,160
And the wall?

172
00:09:19,300 --> 00:09:25,301
Around 2900 BC,
Uruk got a wall nearly six miles long.

173
00:09:25,368 --> 00:09:28,521
Later poets said a king
called Gilgamesh built it.

174
00:09:28,588 --> 00:09:32,745
That story became one of the oldest
great stories ever written.

175
00:09:32,836 --> 00:09:36,234
Did he?
Gilgamesh may have been a real king.

176
00:09:36,301 --> 00:09:38,734
But no writing of his own has ever
been found.

177
00:09:38,801 --> 00:09:40,879
So that one stays a legend.

178
00:09:40,946 --> 00:09:44,279
How many did you guess right?
Tell us in the comments.

179
00:09:44,346 --> 00:09:46,718
So what happened to Uruk?

180
00:09:46,850 --> 00:09:51,311
People lived here for more than 3,500
years after this.

181
00:09:51,378 --> 00:09:55,097
The river shifted away,
and around the seventh century AD,

182
00:09:55,164 --> 00:09:56,597
the last of them left.

183
00:09:56,664 --> 00:10:00,740
Today it's a field of mounds
in the desert of southern Iraq.

184
00:10:00,807 --> 00:10:03,912
But the idea it started
travelled everywhere.

185
00:10:03,979 --> 00:10:06,336
Cities, kings, and writing.

186
00:10:06,403 --> 00:10:12,047
And at the same time, along another river,
far to the west, people were writing too.

187
00:10:12,114 --> 00:10:16,300
That's where our journey goes on Saturday:
the Great Pyramid.

188
00:10:16,367 --> 00:10:20,192
So why did people invent writing?
Most experts agree:

189
00:10:20,259 --> 00:10:24,487
because a city this big could
no longer remember who owed what.

190
00:10:24,554 --> 00:10:26,772
So next time you crumple up a receipt,

191
00:10:26,839 --> 00:10:31,090
remember: you're holding the oldest
kind of writing there is.


--odysseyCAPboundary7--
